# Every `between` as filed, as a reference to another document. One row per reference: which layer
# of which other document a composer says the double counting runs against.
#
# The second `pm:ForeignId` reference
#   The model has two `pm:ForeignId` references. `composition/part_references.sqlc` names an
#   `asrt:Part`; this names `asrt:Elimination/between`. Anyone listing what this model points at
#   in other documents from the parts alone gets half the answer. A refusal reasoned from
#   `elimination`, an amount BPMN has no need for, is not about `between`.
#
# Why no rule reaches it
#   A rule reads only what it needs, so a field no rule reads is under no pressure to exist, and
#   `between` is the one reference to another document that no rule may check: the schema allows
#   one that resolves to nothing, because a group eliminating against a member that files nothing,
#   or against a layer folded into a different composed layer, is ordinary. So no rule can bring it
#   into view, and this relation does.
#
# Its companion is an observation, not a check
#   `eliminations/resolved.sqlc` is the part that lands and this is the whole, and their
#   difference is reported rather than asserted, the way `coupling_search` reports.
#   `checks/unresolved_part.sqlc`'s shape here would accuse the filing the schema names as
#   ordinary.
#
# No foreign key
#   As `composition/part_references.sqlc` has none: a notation may name a document nobody in this
#   corpus filed, and that is a state, not an error.
-- asrt:Elimination/asrt:between, typed asrt:FiledLayer, whose filing is a pm:ForeignId.
SELECT b.composition, b.composed_layer, b.quantity, b.seq,
       b.party, b.notation, b.layer, b.regime
FROM (
    :compose(eliminations/between.sqlc)
) b
